```java
public interface Cache<K, V> {
    V get(K key);
    void put(K key, V value);
    void remove(K key);
    int size();
}
```

A minimal, boring interface is the goal. No mention of eviction, capacity, or thread-safety yet — those are implementation concerns, not API concerns. Keeping return types plain, with no exposed locks or internals, is itself a signal that you separate contract from implementation.

```java
public class BoundedCache<K, V> implements Cache<K, V> {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, V> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final ReentrantLock evictionLock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final int capacity;

    public V get(K key) { return store.get(key); }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        store.put(key, value);
        if (store.size() > capacity) {
            evictionLock.lock();
            try { evictOne(); } finally { evictionLock.unlock(); }
        }
    }
}
```

`get` never takes a lock — it's a plain concurrent-map read, so reads scale across threads. The lock only guards eviction bookkeeping, which is rare relative to reads. This is the same pattern as [designing thread-safe classes](designing-thread-safe-classes): lock the smallest critical section, not the whole class.

```java
public V get(K key, Function<K, V> loader) {
    return store.computeIfAbsent(key, loader);
}
```

Without this, ten concurrent misses for the same cold key trigger ten identical, expensive recomputes — a **thundering herd**. `computeIfAbsent` (or a per-key lock) guarantees only one thread runs the loader; the other nine block briefly and get the same computed value for free.
